.TH RESET 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
reset \- restore the terminal to a sane state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reset
.RI [ term ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reset
puts a terminal that has been left in an unusable mode back into a
normal, usable state.
.PP
It selects the terminal to act on by trying standard error, then
standard output, then
.IR /dev/tty ;
if none of them is a terminal, it writes to standard output.
.PP
First, if the chosen descriptor is a terminal, its attributes are
changed with
.BR tcsetattr (3)
using
.B TCSAFLUSH
(pending input is discarded):
.IP \(bu 2
input: set
.BR BRKINT ,
.BR ICRNL ,
.BR IXON ;
clear
.BR IGNBRK ,
.BR PARMRK ,
.BR ISTRIP ,
.BR INLCR ,
.BR IGNCR ,
.BR IXOFF ;
.IP \(bu
output: set
.BR OPOST ,
.BR ONLCR ;
clear
.BR OCRNL ,
.BR ONOCR ,
.BR ONLRET ,
.BR OFILL ,
.BR OFDEL ;
.IP \(bu
control: 8 data bits, no parity, one stop bit,
.BR CREAD
and
.B CLOCAL
set,
.B HUPCL
cleared;
.IP \(bu
local: set
.BR ICANON ,
.BR ISIG ,
.BR IEXTEN ,
.BR ECHO ,
.BR ECHOE ,
.BR ECHOK ;
clear
.BR ECHONL ,
.BR NOFLSH ,
.BR TOSTOP ;
.IP \(bu
control characters: intr \(haC, quit \(ha\e, erase \(ha? (DEL),
kill \(haU, eof \(haD, start \(haQ, stop \(haS, susp \(haZ,
.B VMIN
1,
.B VTIME
0.
.PP
Other flags, the line speed and the window size are left as they are.
.PP
Then the following VT100/ANSI sequences are written: RIS
.RB ( "ESC c" ),
select US-ASCII into G0
.RB ( "ESC ( B" ),
soft terminal reset DECSTR
.RB ( "ESC [ ! p" ),
show cursor
.RB ( "ESC [ ? 25 h" ),
erase display
.RB ( "ESC [ 2 J" ),
and cursor home
.RB ( "ESC [ H" ).
The same sequences are sent whatever the terminal type; there is no
terminfo or termcap lookup, and
.B TERM
is not consulted.
.PP
The optional
.I term
operand is accepted for compatibility and ignored, as are any other
arguments.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B reset
always exits with status 0, even if the terminal attributes or the
escape sequences could not be written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/tty
Used when neither standard error nor standard output is a terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stty (1),
.BR tty (1),
.BR cfmakeraw (3),
.BR tcsetattr (3)
